        "knowledge_point": "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/assets/e2f149fb4f32fb264e0af6898e7737d570ec90bbff31cf1256eb9bd2636ceab3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
        "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/assets/589f9f524a7c98da61ad6c5001b8d392d89073d5734cd70cfa750d153fe6f7b7.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=-2v.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=4|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-4u.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =-2v-4u \\\\  & =-4u-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-4u-2v.$\n\nSince $u∦v,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =-2$",
